    <att name='institution'>University of British Columbia</att>
    <att name='title'>Spatiotemporal patterns of kelp microbiome across Vancouver Island</att>
    <att name='summary'>The survey aims to investigate the spatial and temporal (seasonal effects) on microbial kelp-tissue communities associated with the Pacific Bull Kelp Nereocystis luetkeana and the Giant Kelp Macrocystis spp. In total, we visited 10 sites across the Salish Sea, Strait of Juan de Fuca and the Central Coast of British Columbia, Canada. Key sites were surveyed on a semi-monthly basis to allow us to detect microbial responses and changes in their diversity and community, and differences between both host species. Bacterial communities will be assessed by collected swab samples of host tissue, followed by 16S analysis and compared to the surrounding water columns. Microbial data will be set into context to sea temperature, overall host health (e.g. blade bleaching) and biofouling of the host organisms (e.g. by bryozoa, amphipoda and other macro organisms). Another aspect of this survey is the differences over time of microbial communities associated with Nereocystis blades, external bulb (pneumatocyst) and internal bulb, and the potential change in communities due to air exposure of the host organism during low tides.</att>
    <att name='project'>Sentinels of Change,Nearshore,Genomics</att>
    <att name='progress'>completed</att>
